The boot hands out the board's devices with the write right,
which a device listed read only, as the counter is, was never granted.
A frame carries no right beyond what its range was granted with,
so a process could map a device register writable that it may only read.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: frame with rights beyond the grant
Witness: fuzz device-region-reads-only

diff --git a/kernel/boot.c b/kernel/boot.c
--- a/kernel/boot.c
+++ b/kernel/boot.c
@@ -168,7 +168,7 @@ struct thread *boot_create_root(void)
                    "the grants of the devices and the blocks lie as their slots do");
     for (unsigned i = BOOT_CAP_DEVICES; i < BOOT_CAP_COUNT; i++) {
         const struct granted_range *g = &boot_granted[GRANT_DEVICES + i - BOOT_CAP_DEVICES];
-        struct cap made = i < BOOT_CAP_RAM_BLOCKS ? cap_to_frame(g->base, g->size, g->rights)
+        struct cap made = i < BOOT_CAP_RAM_BLOCKS ? cap_to_frame(g->base, g->size, g->rights | RIGHT_W)
                                                   : cap_to_untyped(g->base, g->size, g->rights);
         if (cap_store(table, i, &made, NULL) != KERR_OK) {
             kpanic("cannot fill the root task's table");
